    Fix scan_wifi returning no networks: use iw, not NetworkManager
    
    The "Nearby networks" list came back empty on every Pi in the fleet.
    
    get_wifi_scan was the last scan path still built on NetworkManager:
    
        nmcli -t -f SSID,BSSID,... dev wifi list ifname wlan1 --rescan yes
    
    but install.sh ships 99-pi-agent-unmanaged-wifi.conf, which sets
    `unmanaged-devices=interface-name:wl*`. NetworkManager neither scans nor
    reports BSSes for a device it does not manage, so the command exited 0
    and printed nothing, the parse loop iterated zero lines, and the action
    reported `networks: []`. It failed silently precisely because the exit
    code was fine - an empty list is indistinguishable from "nothing in
    range".
    
    get_wifi_scan_detail never had the problem: `iw` talks to cfg80211
    directly and is indifferent to NM's management state.
    
    So get_wifi_scan now goes through the same scan as the detail path and
    flattens the result to the smaller shape the list renders, via
    _flatten_scan_entry. Cache-first, for the same reason wifi_detail_scan
    is: it runs inline on the MQTT message loop and must not sit on a
    multi-second radio scan there. Falls back to a real scan when the cache
    is empty - a freshly booted Pi, or an interface the background loop
    hasn't reached yet, would otherwise show the same empty list this change
    exists to fix.
    
    The frontend contract is unchanged: wifi-scan.js and command-builder.js
    read ssid/bssid/mode/channel/frequency_mhz/band/rate/signal_percent/
    security off each entry, so the flattened entry keeps those names rather
    than the detail scan's, and `rate` keeps its "<n> Mbit/s" string form
    since it is rendered as-is.
    
    Verified by driving get_wifi_scan through the real _parse_scan_bss_block
    with representative `iw scan dump` output (WPA2 2.4GHz, open 5GHz, and an
    IBSS cell): no nmcli invoked, all nine fields present, and mode/band/
    channel/security resolving correctly on each.
    
    This bug predates the asyncio conversion - it dates from ed34e69, which
    added the unmanaged-devices config. Four other NM calls are no-ops on
    these Pis for the same reason (the `managed yes` handback and nmcli
    fallback in disconnect_wifi, and the pair in set_interface_mode); those
    are left alone here.
    
