    Convert pi_agent to asyncio
    
    The agent ran a thread per command and per background loop, each blocked
    in subprocess.run waiting on a CLI tool. On the 4-radios-per-Pi rigs that
    meant four threads mostly parked on `iw`. It now runs on one event loop:
    a task per command, a task per loop, and the waiting done by
    asyncio.create_subprocess_exec.
    
    Scope is the agent only. The backend was deliberately left alone: FastAPI
    already runs its sync `def` endpoints in a threadpool, so they never block
    the event loop, and converting them without also moving SQLModel/SQLite to
    an async driver would make things worse, not better.
    
    New modules:
    
    - agent/proc.py - the single seam every mixin's CLI calls go through.
      `run` wraps create_subprocess_exec with a timeout that kills rather than
      raises, since callers branch on returncode. `run_hard` additionally
      kills the whole process group, which is what dhcpcd/nmcli need: they
      fork helpers that inherit the pipes, so killing only the direct child
      leaves communicate() hanging. Being one seam also means the tests fake
      the entire CLI layer by patching two functions.
    
    - agent/wpa_events.py - wpa_supplicant's control socket, replacing the
      once-a-second `wpa_cli status` poll in _wait_for_wpa_state. Faster, but
      the real gain is detail: the async events carry reason/status codes
      (CTRL-EVENT-ASSOC-REJECT status_code=17) that a state poll never shows,
      so a rejected association returns immediately with a reason instead of
      sitting out its 60s timeout. Surfaced as connect_wifi's new
      failure_reason field. Falls back to polling when the socket isn't
      attachable, so behavior degrades rather than breaks.
    
    Converted throughout:
    
    - threading.Lock -> asyncio.Lock; the guard lock around _wifi_locks is
      gone, since creation has no await in it and is atomic against other
      tasks. The scanner's acquire(blocking=False) became Lock.locked().
    - Detached daemon threads -> tracked tasks. The background scan and
      interface-status loops now gather their per-interface work concurrently
      instead of spawning threads nobody joins, so a failure is reported
      rather than dying silently.
    - streaming.py's relay threads -> tasks reading the child's StreamReader
      and writing an asyncio StreamWriter, so a stalled backend socket parks a
      task instead of pinning a thread.
    - webui_proxy.py's threading.Timer -> a cancellable task.
    - paho -> aiomqtt. handle_command is a coroutine; each message is handled
      as its own task so a slow action doesn't stop the loop pulling the next.
      _dispatch_wifi_command stays fire-and-forget but now keeps a strong task
      reference, since asyncio only holds weak ones and a task nobody
      references can be collected mid-command.
    - __init__ can no longer await, so the startup iptables sweep moved to a
      new start() coroutine, which pi_agent.py drives via asyncio.run.
    
    Testing: the 57-test baseline was carried over and extended to 70 (5 skip
    on Windows, needing AF_UNIX). Because the concurrency assertions were
    written against elapsed time and ordering rather than against
    threading.Lock, they survived the move to asyncio.Lock unchanged in intent
    - the threads became tasks and the acquires became `async with`, but what
    is asserted did not change. Two new ones cover the async-specific hazards:
    four radios' scans overlapping rather than serialising, and dispatched
    tasks being strongly referenced.
    
    Re-ran the mutation battery against the converted code; all four
    regressions are still caught (pending check removed, blocking scan
    restored, wifi_detail_scan back on the message loop, task reference
    dropped).
    
    Not verified on real hardware. The conversion is mechanical in places and
    was checked by a cross-module pass for un-awaited coroutines, but only a
    Pi will exercise the actual subprocess, socket and MQTT paths.
    
