    The agent had no tests. Converting it to asyncio means rewriting the
    locking, the subprocess layer and the MQTT loop at once, so pin the
    current behavior down first and make the conversion verifiable rather
    than hoped-for.
    
    57 tests over four files, all against the sync implementation as it
    stands:
    
      test_wifi_scan.py    - trigger/dump split, cache vs fresh scan, both
                             modes parsing to the same fields, EBUSY handling
      test_wifi_locking.py - who waits for whom on an interface: scan/command
                             starvation, the pending counter, per-interface
                             isolation, dispatch semantics
      test_wifi_connect.py - the wpa_supplicant + dhcpcd sequence, bssid
                             resolution and its fresh-scan fallback, the
                             disconnect teardown chain
      test_commands.py     - handle_command routing, which actions dispatch
                             off-thread, result correlation fields
    
    conftest.py fakes the two things the agent is built on: a FakeCommands
    subprocess double that replays scripted results with realistic durations
    (3s full scan, 20ms trigger, 50ms dump), and a paho Client double that
    records publishes. Timing-based assertions use those durations, so the
    concurrency tests measure real elapsed behavior rather than asserting on
    threading internals - which is what lets them survive the move to
    asyncio.Lock.
    
