Author: Alex[Le Van Thi] <f260105@kaonmedia.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 21 16:12:12 2026 +0700

    Split wifi scans into a locked trigger and a lock-free cache read
    
    The previous commit reduced connect/disconnect stalls by scheduling
    around the background scan loop's lock. This mostly removes the
    contention instead of scheduling around it.
    
    Every scan path drove the radio through a full blocking `iw dev <if>
    scan` (2-5s) while holding the interface's wifi lock - the same lock
    connect_wifi/disconnect_wifi take. That is why a lock was needed at all:
    the loop wasn't reading state, it was commandeering the radio.
    
    `iw` already separates the two halves, the same way Windows' WLAN
    AutoConfig service does for tools that poll it:
    
      iw dev <if> scan trigger  - start a scan, return immediately (ms)
      iw dev <if> scan dump     - read cfg80211's cached BSS list, no radio
    
    So the background loop now holds the lock only for the trigger and reads
    results outside it. Lock occupancy per cycle drops from seconds to
    milliseconds.
    
    Changes:
    
    - Add _scan_trigger/_scan_dump, and a use_cache flag on
      get_wifi_scan_detail selecting between the cache read and the existing
      blocking scan. Both modes emit identical `BSS ...` blocks, so
      _parse_scan_bss_block is unchanged. Responses gain a from_cache field.
    
    - _wifi_background_scan_one triggers under the lock, releases, then dumps
      lock-free. EBUSY on trigger is no longer retried with backoff *while
      holding the lock* (that slept up to 7s); the next cycle is along
      shortly and NetworkManager's own scan fills the same cache anyway.
    
    - The wifi_detail_scan MQTT action serves from cache. It previously ran a
      fresh blocking scan directly on the MQTT network thread, starving
      paho's keepalive for seconds and queueing behind any in-flight
      connect/disconnect. The popup is now served in milliseconds.
    
    - connect_wifi's bssid->ssid resolution checks the cache first and falls
      back to a fresh scan only when the BSSID isn't there (new AP, or an
      interface not scanned since a mode switch), via _find_bssid_in_scan.
    
    - Scan interval 12s -> 6s. Lock contention no longer bounds it; what
      does is RF airtime from the radio going off-channel to probe.
    
    - Add a missing timeout to the `iw dev <if> link` call.
    
    Testing: with iw timings simulated (3s full scan, 20ms trigger, 50ms
    dump), a disconnect arriving 0.2s into a scan cycle stalled 2.80s on the
    old path and 0.00s on the new one. Cache dumps parse to the same fields,
    BSSID lookup is case-insensitive, and a cache miss returns None so
    connect_wifi's fresh-scan fallback engages.
    
    Not verified on real hardware - `scan dump` output format and cache
    retention should be confirmed on a Pi before rollout.
    
