Author: Alex[Le Van Thi] <f260105@kaonmedia.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 21 15:13:09 2026 +0700

    Cut wifi connect/disconnect latency caused by scan lock contention
    
    A user-triggered disconnect_wifi could take tens of seconds to reply.
    The wait was almost entirely contention with the background scan loop
    rather than the disconnect work itself.
    
    _dispatch_wifi_command blocks on the per-interface wifi lock, and the
    background scanner takes that same lock. The scanner ran every 3s while
    a real `iw scan` takes 2-5s (longer with _run_scan_with_busy_retry EBUSY
    backoff), so the lock was held essentially continuously and an arriving
    command almost always had to wait out a scan first.
    
    The scanner's existing non-blocking acquire did not help: it only skips
    when the lock is already held, but a command *queued* on the lock holds
    nothing, so each new scan cycle won the race ahead of it.
    
    Changes:
    
    - Track a per-interface pending-command count, set before the blocking
      acquire in _dispatch_wifi_command and cleared after the command runs.
      _wifi_background_scan_one now checks it first and skips the cycle, so a
      queued command waits for at most the one scan already in flight.
    
    - Raise WIFI_BACKGROUND_SCAN_INTERVAL_SECONDS from 3 to 12, leaving real
      gaps between scans instead of a ~100% lock duty cycle.
    
    - Trim disconnect_wifi's serial timeout chain (terminate 15->5, dhcpcd -k
      15->8, nmcli managed yes 15->8, ip addr flush 15->5), taking the
      worst-case tail from 60s to 26s. These are hang budgets; each call is
      normally sub-second. ip addr flush also moves onto _run_hard_timeout so
      a forked helper inheriting its pipe cannot hang it past its timeout.
    
    - Lower set_interface_mode's nmcli device disconnect timeout from 300s to
      30s. It runs under the same interface lock, so a stuck call there could
      block every later connect/disconnect on that interface for 5 minutes.
    
    - Update two comments that still cited the removed nmcli timeout=300 as
      the rationale for the locking design.
    
    Verified: all three modules byte-compile, and the preemption logic was
    exercised directly - with a scan in flight and a command arriving
    mid-scan, the next scan cycle skips and the pending count clears without
    leaking.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

